//RS /// D-SHIFT1 (ratified 2026-07-01, c7shift): `cursor.take_pattern("…")` reuses
//RS /// the D-PARSESTR1 interpolation-literal-as-pattern grammar (`{hole}` /
//RS /// `{hole:Type}`), but a typed hole isn't a legal ordinary interpolation
//RS /// value expression, so the string-literal argument in THIS ONE call
//RS /// position is parsed as a pattern (`try_str_match_pattern`'s engine, reused
//RS /// not duplicated — I8) instead of an ordinary `Expr::Str`. Parsed specially
//RS /// the same way `.view(a..b)` (above) is: one method name, one fixed
//RS /// argument shape, no second call-argument grammar.
//RS pub const METHOD_TAKE_PATTERN: &str = "take_pattern";
//RS 
//RS /// D-BYTELIT1=B (ratified 2026-08-27, card #2265): byte text reuses the
//RS /// existing typed collection head `[U8]{ "..." }`. ASCII text contributes one
//RS /// byte per character; `\xNN` contributes the written byte. No `b` prefix is
//RS /// added.
//RS /// D-BINPAT1 / D-UNIFYLIT1=A: binary patterns use typed head `[U8]{"…"}`
//RS /// (byte-mode sibling of D-PARSESTR1 string patterns). Each `{name:U4}` hole
//RS /// reads a fixed-width bit field; an endian suffix (`be`/`le`) picks byte
//RS /// order on a multi-byte read; a final `{name:...}` captures remaining bytes.
//RS /// Byte values use the typed list head above; this pattern form remains
//RS /// distinct because its body contains holes.
//RS /// D-BINPAT1: multi-byte big-endian read suffix — `{len:U16be}`.
//RS pub const BINPAT_ENDIAN_BIG: &str = "be";
//RS /// D-BINPAT1: multi-byte little-endian read suffix — `{len:U16le}`.
//RS pub const BINPAT_ENDIAN_LITTLE: &str = "le";
